      "narration": "Increasing b steepens the rise and slides the maximum-growth point ln(a)/b leftward — the population reaches saturation sooner and more abruptly, but the ceiling c is unchanged.",
      "state": {
        "timeMarker": 1.1,
        "growthRate": 2,
        "viewMode": "Population + growth rate f'(t)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turning b down: a gentle climb",
      "narration": "With small b the S stretches out; the maximum-growth point moves far to the right and the curve takes much longer to approach the same carrying capacity.",
      "state": {
        "timeMarker": 6,
        "growthRate": 0.3,
        "viewMode": "Population + growth rate f'(t)"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Logistic growth is fastest not at the beginning and not near the ceiling, but exactly at half the carrying capacity — the inflection point (ln(a)/b, c/2) where the shrinking room to grow finally outweighs the growing population.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Unscaled first-quadrant-style sketch with double-arrowed black axes: t horizontal (arrow both ends), f(t) vertical, no numeric ticks. A dashed orange horizontal line spans the upper third, labeled 'y = c' in orange at its left end, with a thin black leader arrow from the line up-right to the text 'Carrying capacity'. The population curve is a thick dark navy-blue S: it enters flat from the lower left below the t-axis region, crosses the vertical axis at a small open/marked point labeled (0, c/(1+a)) in stacked-fraction math, rises steeply through mid-frame, then flattens and runs just beneath the dashed line to the right edge. A filled navy dot sits on the steepest part, labeled with the stacked coordinate pair (ln(a)/b, c/2) above-left and connected by a leader to 'Point of maximum growth' below-right. The formula f(t) = c/(1 + ae^(-bt)) appears in blue near the upper bend. Interactive additions: a bright marker dot on the curve with a straight tangent segment through it, a faint vertical dropline to the t-axis, a light gray horizontal guide at c/2, and a small readout panel (top-left or bottom-right) listing t, f(t), slope, and gap to c. In derivative view add a lighter teal/green bell curve for f'(t) drawn on the same frame with its peak vertically aligned to the inflection dot. Keep the hand-drawn textbook feel: no gridlines, generous white space, black serif-ish annotation text."
}

